{
  "comment": "@spec ACTION-EXECUTOR-6 @spec ACTION-EXECUTOR-24. The worker's `execute` client and the runner's `/v1/execute` route ship in different images, as do the writer and reader of the mode variable, so the whole route is frozen here. `mode_variable` is the runner-private boot variable (outside `BootEnv`) the worker sets on an executor claim. Every request carries exactly `request_keys`; a key a phase does not use is null. `phases` gives one request and response per phase; `arguments` is the canonical text (action-canonical-arguments.json), never an object. `sequences` lists the only accepted phase orders within one sandbox (a prefix of one is accepted; any other order, including a second `call`, is refused `phase_out_of_order` without dialing). `refusals` is the closed set of route refusals: HTTP status, body, whether the connector may have been dialed, and the pre-dispatch code the worker reports for it. `refused_tool_sequences` are orders refused for a given `call` tool (a forward tool never follows `observe`; a restore always does). `refused_call_consumes`: a `call` refused by preflight still spends the sandbox's one call, so a later `call` is refused without dialing. `connector_not_hosted` refuses a `call` whose connector the grant header cannot ride to (no URL). `bounds`: `list` pagination past `list_pages` is refused, and a `call` result past `call_result_bytes` is answered as an error with no structure (the worker fails it; it is never retried). `absent_route` is a runner image without the route. `status_body` is the executor mode body of `GET /status` and `GET /v1/status`; `other_routes` are refused in executor mode. Read by apps/worker/tests/test_runner_execute_vector.py and runner/tests/test_runner_execute_vector.py, each of which rejects an unknown key. Remediation reads (@spec AUTOMATED-REMEDIATION-12, executor amendments E3, E4 and E6): the `read` request carries exactly `request_keys` plus `read.request_keys_added` (`pointer`, an RFC 6901 pointer); no other phase carries `pointer`, and a request of another phase that does is `invalid_request`. A `read` is `list` then one or more `read`s of one tool with the same canonical arguments and pointer on the read connector, each calling `tools/call` once and answering only the sample at the pointer (`sample` and `value`, kinds and extraction cases in remediation-predicate.json), never the result; no grant rides it. `read.list_response` is the read fixture connector's catalogue; a read whose tool is not advertised with `readOnlyHint: true` in the sandbox's own `list` (`read.not_read_only`) is refused `tool_not_read_only` without dialing. A later read changing any of `read.same_call_fields` is refused `read.changed_call_refusal` (the order check precedes the tool check). An observe-only execution (`list` then repeated `observe`, no `call`) watches a forward action's target for `superseded`; after a second `observe` no `call` is accepted. A sandbox serves at most `read.samples_max` reads or observes, then refuses `read.over_samples_refusal`. A pointer in `read.invalid_pointers` (from remediation-predicate.json) is refused `invalid_request`. A read can never write, so any unrecognized refusal of a read maps to `read.unknown_refusal_worker_code`, never `response_lost`. `remediation_codes.pre_dispatch_added` join the API's closed pre-dispatch codes; `remediation_codes.not_refusals` are sample results, never refusal codes. Also read, for `remediation_codes` only, by apps/api/tests/test_runner_execute_remediation_codes.py.",
